$ goodfetch-collie audit --help
Exports and verifies the user's Audit log

Usage: goodfetch-collie audit [OPTIONS] <COMMAND>

Commands:
  export  Writes the user's Audit log to stdout as JSONL: one entry per line, in sequence, each with the hash that chains it to the line before it, the anchor first when `audit.retention` pruned its beginning. `audit verify --file` verifies what it wrote
  verify  Verifies the user's Audit log, or with `--file` an export of it: every entry's hash chains it to the one before it, from the first or from the anchor a pruning left, and the log's head file names its last. Prints that it is intact, or the first sequence that does not verify, so an edited, deleted, reordered or truncated entry shows

Options:
      --color <WHEN>  When to colour human output; `auto` colours on a terminal unless `NO_COLOR` is set and not empty [default: auto] [possible values: auto, always, never]
  -h, --help          Print help

$ goodfetch-collie audit export --help
Writes the user's Audit log to stdout as JSONL: one entry per line, in sequence, each with the hash that chains it to the line before it, the anchor first when `audit.retention` pruned its beginning. `audit verify --file` verifies what it wrote

Usage: goodfetch-collie audit export [OPTIONS]

Options:
      --json          Prints a failure as JSON on stdout; the entries are JSON either way
      --attached      Runs the call on an Attached engine in this process, even when a Daemon runs
      --daemon        Runs the call on the Daemon of the major, starting it if none runs; its refusal ends the command
      --color <WHEN>  When to colour human output; `auto` colours on a terminal unless `NO_COLOR` is set and not empty [default: auto] [possible values: auto, always, never]
  -h, --help          Print help

$ goodfetch-collie audit verify --help
Verifies the user's Audit log, or with `--file` an export of it: every entry's hash chains it to the one before it, from the first or from the anchor a pruning left, and the log's head file names its last. Prints that it is intact, or the first sequence that does not verify, so an edited, deleted, reordered or truncated entry shows

Usage: goodfetch-collie audit verify [OPTIONS]

Options:
      --file <FILE>   A file `audit export` wrote, verified in place of the log; it has no head file, so its tail is its last line
      --json          Prints the result as JSON
      --attached      Runs the call on an Attached engine in this process, even when a Daemon runs
      --daemon        Runs the call on the Daemon of the major, starting it if none runs; its refusal ends the command
      --color <WHEN>  When to colour human output; `auto` colours on a terminal unless `NO_COLOR` is set and not empty [default: auto] [possible values: auto, always, never]
  -h, --help          Print help

$ goodfetch-collie certs --help
Lists, accepts and forgets the certificate pins of the user's known-certificates store

Usage: goodfetch-collie certs [OPTIONS] <COMMAND>

Commands:
  list    Lists the caller's pins in the known-certificates store: each host whose invalid certificate was accepted on first use, the SHA-256 of its public key, when and by which Run. Another person's pins on a shared Daemon are never listed. A row the engine cannot read is listed without its hash; it stops its host as a changed key
  accept  Pins the changed key that stopped a host in place of its pin: a person's answer, asked at the terminal, and refused with `person_required` under `--json` or when stdin is no terminal
  forget  Removes a host's pin, so its next connection under trust on first use pins again: a person's answer, asked at the terminal, and refused with `person_required` under `--json` or when stdin is no terminal

Options:
      --color <WHEN>  When to colour human output; `auto` colours on a terminal unless `NO_COLOR` is set and not empty [default: auto] [possible values: auto, always, never]
  -h, --help          Print help

