$ goodfetch-collie config explain --help
Shows each effective setting, the layer that set it and the layer that locked it: the built-in default, the Operator policy, user configuration, the project configuration or these flags

Usage: goodfetch-collie config explain [OPTIONS] [KEYS]...

Arguments:
  [KEYS]...  Keys, or prefixes of keys such as `timeout`, to show; every key when none

Options:
      --set <KEY=VALUE>    Sets a setting for this run, as `key=value`, the value read as JSON when it parses as JSON and as text otherwise, a duration as milliseconds or as `45s`, `2m` or `1h`; repeatable
      --settings <FILE|->  Reads settings for this run from a JSON object, nested by key, in FILE, or from standard input for `-`; `--set` beats it
      --project <PROJECT>  Reads this project configuration instead of the `goodfetch.toml` found from the working folder upward
      --json               Prints the result as JSON
      --daemon             Asks the Daemon of the major, starting it if none runs, instead of resolving in this process
      --attached           Resolves in this process, as it does unless `--daemon` is given
      --color <WHEN>       When to colour human output; `auto` colours on a terminal unless `NO_COLOR` is set and not empty [default: auto] [possible values: auto, always, never]
  -h, --help               Print help

$ goodfetch-collie daemon --help
Starts, stops and shows the Daemon of this executable's major, which both Products share

Usage: goodfetch-collie daemon [OPTIONS] <COMMAND>

Commands:
  start                Starts the Daemon, the major's pick, unless one runs
  stop                 Stops the Daemon; its live Runs stop with it
  restart              Stops the Daemon, then starts the major's pick
  status               Shows whether the Daemon runs, as which Product, and its Clients
  health               Says whether the Daemon of the major, or the foreground Daemon at `daemon.address`, is ready to take a run, as its ops listener's `/readyz` does, for `exec` probes and systemd: exits 0 when it is, 1 when it is not or none answers
  install              Registers the major's Daemon as a per-user service, which starts the major's pick: a launch agent on macOS and a systemd user unit on Linux, each started by the first connection to its socket, and a logon task on Windows
  uninstall            Removes the major's per-user service, stopping its Daemon
  rotate-token         Replaces the persistent token of the major's Daemon and the HTTP listener's token: an installed service's Daemon stops with every Connection that opened with the old one, and a Daemon started after it refuses the old one
  http-listener-token  Prints the token the Daemon's HTTP listener takes, for a workflow tool's configuration: on stdout, and nowhere else
  run                  Runs the Daemon in the foreground, for Docker, Kubernetes and systemd: as this executable's Product, listening on `daemon.address`, with no runtime file and no idle stop, logging JSON lines to stdout; on `SIGTERM` it takes no run, checkpoints each run as resumable and exits within `daemon.drain_grace`

Options:
      --color <WHEN>  When to colour human output; `auto` colours on a terminal unless `NO_COLOR` is set and not empty [default: auto] [possible values: auto, always, never]
  -h, --help          Print help

