$ goodfetch-collie run --help
Calls the catalog's `run` operations

Usage: goodfetch-collie run [OPTIONS] <COMMAND>

Commands:
  new-page     Opens a page in the Run's default Session, which the first call opens with the Run's settings and every later call shares; `page.session()` reaches it. The first page launches the Run's browser, so a missing browser build raises its code from this call
  new-session  Opens a Session of the Run's own: its own browser context, with its own cookies, storage, identity and proxy, sharing the Run's browser process, so two Sessions of a Run never share a login. It opens from `source`, or else from the Run's `session.source`, or fresh; `session.source` in its own `settings` is refused with `setting_not_applicable`. The Run's first Session launches its browser, so a missing browser build raises its code from this call
  start        Starts a Run its program drives, and answers once it is started. A Run ID names the Run for good: a start with an existing Run ID returns that Run as it is when the caller and the inputs match, and fails with `run_conflict` otherwise
  finish       Lets a Run finish and answers its Run report: a Run its program drives ends at once, `finished` and `complete`; a crawl ends once its work items are done, and a keep-alive crawl (`crawl.keep_alive`), which waits for new work while nothing is left, ends once this call lets it and its work items are done. A Run something stopped first answers its report saying `stopped`. A Run that ended `failed` fails the call with the error that failed it, and a write at its end that failed, such as its Plan file's, with that write's error. Cancelling the call, or its `timeout` passing, which fails it with `deadline_exceeded`, ends only the wait
  stop         Stops a Run at its next safe point with a Checkpoint, `stopped` and `incomplete` as an abort by its program, resumable by its Run ID, and answers its Run report; an ended Run's report is answered as it is. The wait is bounded by `timeout.close`, or the call's `timeout`: past it, the call fails with `deadline_exceeded` and the Run still stops at its next safe point. A Run that ended `failed` fails the call as `run.finish` does
  abort        Stops a Run at its next safe point, `stopped` and `incomplete`: no attempt in flight commits, and its work items return to pending. Answers its Run report; an ended Run's report is answered as it is. A Run whose engine died is stopped by this engine, which takes its writer role at the next run epoch; a Run another engine writes is stopped by that engine, the owner the Run index names
  usage        Answers what a Run used, as the Usage ledger charged it: its model calls and tokens, browser time, proxy bytes, requests, paid solves and Paid fetches, each Limit that applies to it with what is left of it, and whether it is uncapped. An ended Run answers its report's Usage
  progress     Reads a Run's progress: what it does and why it waits. Without `after`, it answers at once; given `after`, the position of the value the reader last read, it waits for a change and answers the latest value, so values in between are coalesced. The value whose `last` is true ends the sequence: the Run ended, and its `lifecycle` and `stop_reason` say how, or no engine works it (`writer: none`) until a start with its Run ID resumes it. A Run another engine works is read through that engine, the owner the Run index names, and one no engine works from its committed state; a read never takes the Run's writer lock. A Run ID the Run index does not list fails with `run_not_found`
  report       Reads a Run's report: at once for a Run that ended, and at its end for a live one, which the read never ends. A Run another engine works is read through that engine, and one no engine works from its committed state, never taking its writer lock. A Run that has no live writer and has not ended fails with `run_interrupted`, whose `fix` names a start with its Run ID, and a Run ID the Run index does not list with `run_not_found`
  list         Lists the Runs of the Run index of the user and major, of either Product (ADRs 0031 and 0098): each row with its tags, Lifecycle, Verdict, Completeness, start and end, the engine that owns it while it is live, and its presence, a Run whose state is gone `missing` and one discarded on purpose `erased`, never left out. The engine filters by tags, the Recipe, Schedule, occurrence and trigger its reserved `goodfetch.` tags name, Product, each axis, start and end time, and a text found in its Run ID or tags, and sorts by the key given; Watch runs are left out unless `watch_runs` asks for them (ADR 0036)
  checkpoint   Writes a Checkpoint of a live Run now, through SQLite's online backup, where `location` says or where the engine keeps the Run's Checkpoints, and answers it once its file is complete, with its size, digest, run epoch and journal position. A Checkpoint the engine cannot write fails with `file_write_failed` and leaves no file
  pause        Pauses a live Run: checkpoints it, then stops it at its next safe point, `stopped` with the stop reason `pause` and resumable by its Run ID with `run.resume`, after a restart too. Answers its Run report, which names the Checkpoint
  migrate      Migrates a live Run: checkpoints it where `location` says or where the engine keeps its Checkpoints, then stops it at its next safe point, `stopped` with the stop reason `migrate`, to be resumed from that Checkpoint on another machine. Answers its Run report, which names the Checkpoint
  resume       Resumes an interrupted Run from its committed state, its run file on this machine or the Checkpoint `checkpoint` names: it takes the Run's writer lock and the next run epoch, so every write of an earlier writer is refused, and redoes the work since that state; its recorded Clean-up rules digest is kept. A live Run is answered as it is, `existing`; a finished one as it is. A Run bound to another machine fails with `session_bound_to_machine`, and a Checkpoint of a format version this engine cannot read with `format_version_unsupported`

Options:
      --color <WHEN>  When to colour human output; `auto` colours on a terminal unless `NO_COLOR` is set and not empty [default: auto] [possible values: auto, always, never]
  -h, --help          Print help

